<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
        <!--
        An executable rather than a library, so the lane can run the assertions in Program.cs
        instead of only compiling them. A library would prove the samples compile and prove
        nothing about what ContributeServices actually registers.
        -->
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <RootNamespace>CustomValidatorPluginConsumer</RootNamespace>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
    Declared as properties so CI can point this at the two versions it just packed without
    editing a tracked file. Neither default is produced by anything, which is the point: a
    developer who builds this without packing first gets a restore failure naming a version
    that does not exist, rather than a quiet success against whatever was lying around.

      ./build-dms.ps1 Build -Configuration Release
      ./build-dms.ps1 Package -PackageTarget CustomValidation -Configuration Release
      ./build-dms.ps1 Package -PackageTarget Plugins -Configuration Release

      Import-Module ./package-helpers.psm1
      ./eng/verification/Invoke-CustomValidatorPluginConsumerCheck.ps1 `
        -CustomValidationPackageVersion (Get-CustomValidationContractVersion) `
        -PluginsPackageVersion (Get-PluginsContractVersion) `
        -NuGetPackagesDirectory (Join-Path ([System.IO.Path]::GetTempPath()) "nuget-verify-$([guid]::NewGuid().ToString('N'))")

    Each contract's version is its own surface version and not the DMS release version: the plugin
    contract declares it in src/plugins/Directory.Build.props and the custom-validation contract in
    its own csproj, and the two readers above read those same files. Neither pack takes a
    -DMSVersion; the custom-validation target ignores one. The two are passed separately because
    they move independently of each other, not because either derives from the release.

    Both are pinned exactly, in [brackets]. A bare version is a floor, not a pin, so with more
    than one nupkg in the local folder feed NuGet resolves a different one and warns via NU1603
    instead of failing, which would let this check validate an artifact other than the one just
    packed.
  -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <CustomValidationPackageVersion Condition="'$(CustomValidationPackageVersion)' == ''"
            >0.0.0-local</CustomValidationPackageVersion
        >
        <PluginsPackageVersion Condition="'$(PluginsPackageVersion)' == ''"
            >0.0.0-local</PluginsPackageVersion
        >
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
    Deliberately outside central package management, and carrying no ProjectReference into
    src/ - a project reference would defeat the entire point of the check. It is in no .sln and
    nothing in the repository builds it except the runner beside it.

    This is the third verification consumer and it is deliberately separate from the two that
    exist, because it makes a statement neither of them can:

      - eng/verification/CustomValidationConsumer proves EdFi.Api.CustomValidation compiles for
        a consumer whose closure is the contract plus DI and Options, with no plugin contract in
        sight. That isolation is the check, so adding EdFi.Api.Plugins to it would remove the
        property it exists to hold. Its CI step also runs before the plugin package is packed.
      - eng/verification/PluginsConsumer proves the same for EdFi.Api.Plugins alone.
      - This project proves the two contracts COMPOSE: that the samples CUSTOM-VALIDATION.md
        publishes compile as an outside implementer would compile them, against the two packed
        contracts and nothing else from Ed-Fi.

    The five references below are exactly what the samples use, and no more. Declaring exactly
    that is what makes this project match an external implementer rather than one with a richer
    closure. Nothing here declares a FrameworkReference: a plugin is served the host's shared
    framework at load time, and naming one here would put assemblies in this project's compile
    closure that an implementer's own project would not have.
  -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="EdFi.Api.CustomValidation" Version="[$(CustomValidationPackageVersion)]" />
        <PackageReference Include="EdFi.Api.Plugins" Version="[$(PluginsPackageVersion)]" />
        <!--
      The three Microsoft.Extensions packages the samples need and neither contract supplies.
      EdFi.Api.CustomValidation declares no dependency at all, and EdFi.Api.Plugins declares
      only the two Abstractions assemblies its own hook signature names, so:

        - Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions carries IServiceCollection,
          ServiceDescriptor and TryAddEnumerable. EdFi.Api.Plugins declares it too; naming it
          here is what keeps this project behaving like an outside one rather than one that
          happens to inherit what it needs.
        - Microsoft.Extensions.Options carries IOptions<T> and the Action<TOptions> overload of
          Configure.
        - Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions carries
          OptionsConfigurationServiceCollectionExtensions, which is the section-binding overload
          services.Configure<T>(configuration.GetSection(...)), and it brings
          Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Binder transitively, which is what actually binds.
          This is the one the sibling custom-validation consumer does not declare, correctly:
          its sample binds through the Action<TOptions> overload alone.

      These versions are deliberately independent of src/Directory.Packages.props and of
      Dependabot, whose nuget scope is directory: src/. An outside implementer picks their own,
      and this project exists to behave like one. What they cannot go below is the floor
      EdFi.Api.Plugins declares, since NuGet prefers a version declared here over the same
      package pulled in transitively.
    -->
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.1" />
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="10.0.1" />
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" Version="10.0.1" />
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
